1. What is a Linked List? (Core Concept)A Linked List is a linear data structure where elements are not stored in contiguous memory locations. Instead, each element is a separate object called a Node, and individual nodes are linked together dynamically using Pointers (memory addresses).Node Structure: Each node consists of two essential parts:Data: The actual value or payload stored in the node.Next (Pointer): The memory address referencing the next node in the sequence.Head Pointer: A pointer pointing directly to the very first node in the list. If the list is empty, Head is nullptr (NULL).Tail Node: The final node in the list points to nullptr, marking the termination of the sequence.Dynamic Sizing: Memory is allocated on the heap one node at a time during runtime, requiring no predefined capacity or memory reallocation copying.2. Memory Layout: Array vs. Linked ListArray (Contiguous):$$\begin{array}{\|c\|c\|c\|c\|}   \hline   \text{10 } (\text{0x1000}) & \text{20 } (\text{0x1004}) & \text{30 } (\text{0x1008}) & \text{40 } (\text{0x100C}) \\   \hline   \end{array}$$Linked List (Scattered in Heap Memory):$$[\text{Head: 0x4A10}] \longrightarrow [\text{10 } \vert{} \text{ 0x8C20}] \longrightarrow [\text{20 } \vert{} \text{ 0x1F00}] \longrightarrow [\text{30 } \vert{} \text{ nullptr}]$$3. Time Complexity & OperationsInsertion at Head: $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Point new node to current head and reassign head.Deletion at Head: $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Move head forward and deallocate the old node.Access / Search by Index: $\mathcal{O}(N)$ — Must traverse sequentially node-by-node starting from Head (no direct random indexing).Insertion / Deletion at End or Middle: $\mathcal{O}(N)$ — Requires traversing to the target position, followed by an $\mathcal{O}(1)$ pointer reconnection.4. Complete C++ Implementation (Singly Linked List)C++#include <iostream>

// Node definition
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;

    Node(int val) : data(val), next(nullptr) {}
};

class LinkedList {
private:
    Node* head;

public:
    LinkedList() : head(nullptr) {}

    ~LinkedList() {
        Node* current = head;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            Node* nextNode = current->next;
            delete current;
            current = nextNode;
        }
        head = nullptr;
    }

    // Insert a new node at the beginning: O(1)
    void InsertAtHead(int value) {
        Node* newNode = new Node(value);
        newNode->next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }

    // Insert a new node at the end: O(N)
    void InsertAtEnd(int value) {
        Node* newNode = new Node(value);
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = newNode;
            return;
        }

        Node* temp = head;
        while (temp->next != nullptr) {
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        temp->next = newNode;
    }

    // Delete a node by value: O(N)
    bool Delete(int value) {
        if (head == nullptr) return false;

        // Case 1: The node to delete is the Head
        if (head->data == value) {
            Node* temp = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete temp;
            return true;
        }

        // Case 2: Node is in the middle or end
        Node* current = head;
        while (current->next != nullptr && current->next->data != value) {
            current = current->next;
        }

        if (current->next == nullptr) {
            return false; // Value not found
        }

        Node* nodeToDelete = current->next;
        current->next = current->next->next;
        delete nodeToDelete;
        return true;
    }

    // Print all elements
    void Print() const {
        Node* temp = head;
        std::cout << "List: ";
        while (temp != nullptr) {
            std::cout << temp->data << " -> ";
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        std::cout << "nullptr\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;

    // Insertion
    list.InsertAtHead(30);
    list.InsertAtHead(20);
    list.InsertAtHead(10); // List: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> nullptr
    list.InsertAtEnd(40);   // List: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> nullptr

    list.Print();

    // Deletion
    list.Delete(20); // Removes middle node
    list.Print();   // List: 10 -> 30 -> 40 -> nullptr

    list.Delete(10); // Removes head node
    list.Print();   // List: 30 -> 40 -> nullptr

    return 0;
}
5. SummaryNo Resizing Overhead: Expands and contracts seamlessly with zero block copying, avoiding vector reallocation spikes.Efficient Head Operations: Insertion and deletion at the front take guaranteed $\mathcal{O}(1)$ time without shifting elements.Trade-offs: Extra memory overhead per node for storing pointer addresses, slower traversal speeds due to lower CPU cache locality, and lack of $\mathcal{O}(1)$ random indexing.
